## PUBLIC ADMINISTRATION THEORY

### Bovens, M. (2007)

**Full citation:** Bovens, M. (2007). Analysing and assessing accountability: A conceptual framework. *European Law Journal*, *13*(4), 447–468.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — accountability theory  
**Claim:** Accountability is a social relationship in which an actor is obliged to explain and justify conduct to a forum, which may impose consequences — distinguishing between accountability as a mechanism and accountability as a virtue.  
**Where used:** §1.4 (public administration accountability theory tradition); §4 architecture (ex-post accountability and redress phase); §8 generalisation  
**Shared anchor:** [[Bovens_2007]]

---

### Lipsky, M. (1980)

**Full citation:** Lipsky, M. (1980). *Street-level bureaucracy: Dilemmas of the individual in public services*. Russell Sage Foundation.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — street-level bureaucracy  
**Claim:** Front-line public servants exercise discretion that shapes policy outcomes in ways that cannot be fully controlled from above — when this discretion is automated, the resulting systems inherit institutional priorities that may not match statutory mandates.  
**Where used:** §1.3 (NPM substrate — Lipsky on displacement of frontline discretion under managerialist reform); §3 (street-level bureaucracy as the pre-agentic analogue)

---

### Hood, C. (1991)

**Full citation:** Hood, C. (1991). A public management for all seasons? *Public Administration*, *69*(1), 3–19.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — New Public Management  
**Claim:** New Public Management reforms trained state institutions to measure success through efficiency, throughput, and cost-recovery proxies — creating an institutional substrate in which proxy optimisation becomes the default design logic.  
**Where used:** §1.3 (NPM as institutional substrate within which administrative goal drift operates)

---

### Merton, R. K. (1957)

**Full citation:** Merton, R. K. (1957). Bureaucratic structure and personality. In *Social theory and social structure* (pp. 195–206). Free Press. (Original work published 1940)  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — goal displacement  
**Claim:** Bureaucratic goal displacement occurs when instrumental means are substituted for the original goals — compliance with procedures replaces achievement of the underlying statutory purpose.  
**Where used:** §4.5 institutional Goodhart (goal drift combines Mertonian goal displacement at operator level with proxy optimisation at system level)

---

### Manheim, D., & Garrabrant, S. (2019)

**Full citation:** Manheim, D., & Garrabrant, S. (2019). Categorizing variants of Goodhart's law. *arXiv:1803.04585v4*. https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04585  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — Goodhart's law variants  
**Claim:** When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure — categorising the variants of this pathology reveals how different types of proxy optimisation produce different failure modes.  
**Where used:** §4.5 (institutional Goodhart — the system-level variant of Goodhart's law applied to administrative proxy optimisation)

