## AGENTIC AI THEORY

### Kasirzadeh, A., & Gabriel, I. (2025)

**Full citation:** Kasirzadeh, A., & Gabriel, I. (2025). Characterizing AI agents for alignment and governance. [Manuscript].  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — agentic AI characterisation  
**Claim:** Agentic AI systems act with degrees of autonomy, memory, and tool access that make them distinct from predictive models — their governance requires frameworks that address action, not just output.  
**Where used:** §1.1 (the shift from output to action); §1.2 (Aadhaar and Robodebt as agentic at system level)

---

### Kapoor, S., Kolt, N., & Lazar, S. (2025)

**Full citation:** Kapoor, S., Kolt, N., & Lazar, S. (2025). Build agent advocates, not platform agents. *Proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025)*, PMLR 267.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — principal orientation  
**Claim:** AI agents structurally serve either the platforms that deploy them (platform agents) or the users they represent (agent advocates) — the public sector analogue is whether administrative systems serve citizens as rights-bearing subjects or departments as efficiency-seeking principals.  
**Where used:** §1.3 (principal-orientation problem)

---

### Baird, A., & Maruping, L. M. (2021)

**Full citation:** Baird, A., & Maruping, L. M. (2021). The next generation of research on IS use: A theoretical framework of delegation to and from agentic IS artifacts. *MIS Quarterly*, *45*(1), 315–341.  
**Role:** Theoretical anchor — IS delegation to agentic artefacts  
**Claim:** Delegation to agentic IS artefacts creates accountability gaps because the artefact acts on behalf of principals in ways that may not align with their original intentions.  
**Where used:** §1.2 (Aadhaar and Robodebt as agentic at system level — IS theoretical warrant for treating deterministic systems as agentic)

